<TextPage title="Ocean Safety" seoTitle="Ocean Safety for Surf, Dive & Freedive Travel | Surf and Dive" description="Clear, non-alarmist ocean safety guidance for surf, scuba and freediving travellers — skill level, reefs, currents, certified operators, buddy systems and local conditions." eyebrow="Safety" lede="Good decisions make good ocean holidays. The principles we apply across every Surf and Dive guide." path="/ocean-safety/">
  <p>These principles are general information, not a substitute for qualified instruction, local advice or official safety guidance. Never let a destination guide — ours included — override what you see in the water or what local professionals tell you.</p>

  <h2>Surf</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Skill level:</strong> surf waves suited to your ability. Famous does not mean beginner-friendly.</li>
    <li><strong>Reef breaks:</strong> ask about depth at each stage of the tide, learn safe entry and exit points and consider reef boots.</li>
    <li><strong>Currents:</strong> learn to recognise rip currents and channels, and how to move out of them.</li>
    <li><strong>Local conditions:</strong> use local guides at unfamiliar breaks; swell, wind and tide change spots hour by hour.</li>
    <li><strong>Lifeguards:</strong> where present, follow their flags and advice. Where absent, never surf alone.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><a href="/guides/ocean-safety-luxury-surf-travellers/">Ocean Safety: What Every Luxury Surf Traveller Should Know</a></p>

  <h2>Scuba diving</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Certified operators:</strong> dive with qualified, reputable operators and dive within your training.</li>
    <li><strong>Equipment standards:</strong> well-maintained equipment and a proper pre-dive check.</li>
    <li><strong>Dive planning:</strong> conservative profiles, sensible guide ratios and clear briefings.</li>
    <li><strong>Weather conditions:</strong> current, swell and visibility can change quickly — be willing to cancel.</li>
    <li><strong>Local regulations:</strong> marine-park rules and the no-fly interval after diving.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Freediving</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Never freedive alone.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Use a trained buddy</strong> who watches every dive from the surface.</li>
    <li><strong>Do not compete</strong> with depth or breath-hold records.</li>
    <li><strong>Do not hyperventilate.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Follow qualified instruction</strong> from a recognised training organisation.</li>
    <li><strong>Respect local conditions</strong> — current, wind, boat traffic and temperature.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><a href="/guides/ocean-safety-freediving-buddy-systems/">Ocean Safety: Freediving, Buddy Systems and Local Conditions</a></p>

  <h2>Wildlife</h2>
  <p>Keep your distance, never touch or feed animals and follow local codes of conduct. A respectful encounter is safer for you and for the animal.</p>

  <h2>Insurance and health</h2>
  <p>Check that your travel insurance explicitly covers surfing, diving or freediving and, for remote destinations, medical evacuation. Seek current health advice for your destination before travel.</p>
</TextPage>
